Q1. Deadlock Debugging (10 Marks)
Problem Identification

In the given pseudo-code:

Process P1

Locks Resource R1
Waits for 100 ms
Requests Resource R2

Process P2

Locks Resource R2
Waits for 100 ms
Requests Resource R1

Here, P1 is holding R1 and waiting for R2, while P2 is holding R2 and waiting for R1. Neither process releases its resource, causing both to wait forever.

Deadlock Condition

The deadlock occurs because of the Circular Wait condition.

The four Coffman conditions are present:

Mutual Exclusion
Hold and Wait
No Preemption
Circular Wait

Since all four conditions are satisfied, the system enters a deadlock.

Optimization

To eliminate deadlock, both processes should acquire resources in the same order.

Example:

P1: Lock R1 → Lock R2

P2: Lock R1 → Lock R2

This removes the circular wait condition.

Another solution is to use resource ordering or try-lock mechanisms.

Analysis

Using a fixed resource allocation order prevents deadlock, improves resource utilization, and ensures smooth execution of processes.

Q2. CPU Utilization Problem (10 Marks)
Problem Identification

The CPU utilization is only 30% even though many processes are ready.

The scheduler log shows:

Ready → Waiting → Ready → Waiting

This indicates that processes spend most of their time waiting for I/O operations.

Debugging

The CPU becomes idle because processes are blocked while waiting for disk or network operations.

Frequent transitions between Ready and Waiting also increase context switching overhead.

As a result:

CPU remains idle.
Throughput decreases.
Response time increases.
Optimization

Optimization 1
Increase the degree of multiprogramming so that when one process waits for I/O, another process can use the CPU.

Optimization 2
Use asynchronous I/O or buffering to reduce waiting time.

Optimization 3
Improve scheduling using Multilevel Feedback Queue or Priority Scheduling.

Analysis

Reducing I/O waiting keeps the CPU busy, increases utilization, improves throughput, and reduces response time.

Q3. Memory Bottleneck Debugging (10 Marks)
Problem Identification

Memory Usage:

Code = 5 MB
Heap = 150 MB
Stack = 1 MB per thread
Threads = 25
RAM = 2 GB

The application experiences frequent page faults.

Cause

Each thread requires stack memory.

25 × 1 MB = 25 MB Stack

Large heap allocation and multiple threads increase memory usage.

Frequent page faults occur because required pages are not available in RAM.

Optimization

Optimization 1
Reduce the number of threads using a thread pool.

Optimization 2
Optimize heap memory by freeing unused memory.

Optimization 3
Increase physical memory or reduce unnecessary allocations.

Analysis

Reducing page faults improves execution speed, memory efficiency, and overall system performance.

Q4. Starvation Debugging (10 Marks)
Problem Identification

Scheduler:

Q1 → Interactive

Q2 → System

Q3 → Batch

P5 belongs to Q3.

Since Q1 always has processes, P5 never gets CPU time.

Cause

This is Starvation.

Low-priority processes continuously wait because higher-priority queues always execute first.

Optimization

Use Aging.

Aging gradually increases the priority of waiting processes.

Eventually, P5 moves to a higher-priority queue and gets CPU time.

Another solution is to use a Multilevel Feedback Queue.

Analysis

Aging ensures fairness, prevents starvation, and improves CPU utilization.

Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging (10 Marks)
Problem Identification

Given:

Context Switches = 20,000/sec
Burst Time = 2 ms
Time Quantum = 1 ms
High I/O frequency
Cause

The time quantum is smaller than the burst time.

Every process is interrupted before completion.

High I/O also causes frequent switching.

This results in excessive context switches.

Optimization

Optimization 1
Increase the time quantum from 1 ms to 5–10 ms.

This allows processes to complete more work before switching.

Optimization 2
Reduce unnecessary thread creation and optimize I/O operations.

Use asynchronous I/O where possible.

Analysis

Benefits:

Fewer context switches.
Lower CPU overhead.
Better throughput.
Improved CPU utilization.
Faster execution.
